\section{No design argument}{
Deliberately none, in either method. The bound holds for every visit
schedule, and for every dropout pattern too, since dropout only ever raises
the sample size. Passing a design would be passing something the answer does
not depend on --- the same reason \code{\link[=slope_effect_size]{slope_effect_size()}} refuses an
\code{effectiveness} argument.
}

\section{How tight it is}{
\code{slope_sample_size(params, design, ...)$n} is greater than or equal to this
for every \code{design}, strictly so before rounding. The bound is approached as
the schedule becomes long and dense, and after \code{ceiling()} a long enough
schedule reaches it exactly: on \code{slpower1} at the paper's 33\% effectiveness
the floor is 236, and a fifty-year trial with visits every five weeks needs
236. Ordinary schedules are nowhere near --- the paper's own two-year
three-visit design needs 712, three times the floor.

\code{effectiveness} is not a detail here. Sample size scales as
\code{effectiveness^-2}, so a floor computed at the default 0.25 says nothing
about a trial powered for a 33\% effect.
}

\section{The reference slope}{


The target treatment effect is defined relative to a reference slope, which
depends on the data the parameters came from:

\describe{
\item{\code{comparator = "none"}}{A treatment that is 100\% effective halts change
entirely; the reference slope is zero.}
\item{\code{comparator = "healthy"}}{A treatment that is 100\% effective reduces
change to the rate seen in a disease-free population; the reference slope
is that of the healthy controls.}
\item{\code{comparator = "treated"} with \code{target = "observed"}}{The planned trial
targets the same treatment effect as the previous trial.}
\item{\code{comparator = "treated"} with \code{target = "effectiveness"}}{The previous
trial's treated arm is ignored and the reference slope is zero, as in the
Stata command's default behaviour for trial data.}
}
}

